{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791441591,"nonce":"ak9k2NmLvbv4VaKF0r1QQJ/ZCGU=","title":"HIR: pure-read aggregate splitting rejects calls after the last field extraction","message":"nbody_modified advance loads a 48-byte nested tuple from the pair array, immediately extracts all six scalar fields, then calls zb_list_expect_len_f64. The aggregate never needs to escape. Both the entry body and the actual advance_resume0 OSR body copy all six words to a stack temporary, then read those words back to unpack the pair.\n\naggregate_split::try_build_plan_for_load walks to the end of the block and rejects any memory-side-effecting instruction. In this pure-read case it reaches the later length-check call and returns None even though every extraction from the aggregate is before that call. Cranelift's by-reference aggregate Load lowering then materializes the snapshot with stack stores. The native dump retains them.\n\nImplement a bounded pure-read analysis that proves all aggregate uses and preserves snapshot semantics. A call after the final extraction need not prevent splitting, but calls or potentially aliasing writes before a required extraction must remain barriers. Preserve cross-block escape checks, volatile/atomic behavior and nested struct layout. Pin nested extraction followed by a call, mutation before extraction, and escaping aggregates in regression tests. Confirm both entry and OSR native bodies lose the temporary, then measure normal LLVM-off nbody wall and kernel time on the NUC.\n\nEvidence: nbody_modified/hir/fn-advance-body.hir lines 61-85; extracted/advance-0.vcode lines 340-365; extracted/advance_resume0-0.vcode around line 350. Source crates/compiler/src/aggregate_split.rs::try_build_plan_for_load and cranelift_backend.rs aggregate Load lowering.\n\nCaptured on the NUC (i5-1250P, x86-64) using saved fdf137cb52c3dc468a8c52d5bc88b1a6431a3a1c binaries, LLVM disabled, normal interpreter-first tiering. No builds ran. This is code-quality evidence, not a measured speedup. ZYNTAX_DUMP_CRANELIFT is input CLIF, before Cranelift optimization; the paired VCode dump is generated native code. OSR tracing was enabled, so total function call counts include instrumentation and cold paths.\n\nArtifacts: NUC /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb and local /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb-local. Script /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit.py. Local extracted/*.clif and *.vcode split the named dumps from stderr.log; optimized HIR is in each case's hir directory.","files":null}]}